// Reading a buffer after handing it to `str_buf_append`.
//
// The call takes the buffer and releases the old one when it reallocates, so the
// argument is dead the moment the call returns. A later read is a read of bytes
// that have been freed, and it used to be exactly that: the call released nothing,
// so the read happened to work and the buffer leaked instead.
//
// The diagnostic names the call rather than the buffer, because the call is where
// the buffer was given up, and it offers both ways forward. Assigning into the
// variable is the usual one, and it works: the flag the call sets is cleared by
// the assignment that receives the result, so a second append to the same buffer
// is not an error.
//
// `use_after_move` is the same diagnostic `move` produces, and it is the same
// check, which is why the note says what it says.
int main() {
    var b = str_buf_new(8);
    var n = str_buf_append(b, "one");
    Console.WriteLog(n);
    // The buffer belongs to `n` now. Reading `b` here is a use of freed bytes.
    Console.WriteLog(len(b));
    return 0;
}